[Principle] Adding more scales covers the frequency range from local details to global illumination more fully.
[Eq. 2-93] R_MSR_i = Σk ωk·{ln Ii − ln[Ii * Gk]}
[Eq. 2-94] ωk = 1/N (here N=5, each scale equally weighted 1/5)
[Parameters] This demo uses σ = {15, 40, 80, 150, 250}
[Effect] Smoother and more robust than the three-scale version, at the cost of more computation.

[Principle] Enhancing each channel independently in MSR tends to shift the overall colors away from the originals (grayish or color cast); MSRCR introduces the color restoration factor Ci to adjust the enhancement strength of each channel.
[Eq. 2-95] Ci(x,y) = f[ Ii(x,y) / Σj Ij(x,y) ], where f is a transform function (usually linear or logarithmic; linear in this implementation)
[Eq. 2-96] R_MSRCR_i = Σk Ci·ωk·{ln Ii − ln[Ii * Gk]}
[Parameters] This demo uses σ = {15, 80, 250}
[Effect] Improves color fidelity on top of MSR enhancement and alleviates color distortion.
